* TDM Operands::             Operands
@end menu

@node TDM Chars
@subsection Special Characters

@cindex line comment character, TDM
@cindex TDM line comment character
@samp{;} starts a comment anywhere on a line.  @samp{#} starts a comment
at the beginning of a line.  There is no statement separator character.

@node TDM Registers
@subsection Register Names

@cindex TDM registers
@cindex register names, TDM
The registers are @code{$pc}, @code{$sp}, @code{$r0} and @code{$r1}.
Register names are not case sensitive.  @samp{$} can't appear in symbol
names.

@node TDM Operands
@subsection Operands

@cindex TDM operands
@cindex TDM addressing modes
Every instruction has a register operand and, except for the single
operand instructions, a second operand that is either a memory reference
or an immediate:

@table @code
@item [$pc+@var{e}]
@itemx [$sp+@var{e}]
@itemx [$r0+@var{e}]
@itemx [@var{e}]
The memory location at a register plus @var{e}, or at @var{e}.

@item $pc+@var{e}
@itemx $sp+@var{e}
@itemx $r0+@var{e}
@itemx @var{e}
The value of a register plus @var{e}, or @var{e} itself.
@end table

@var{e} is a signed 6-bit value, @minus{}32 to 31.  Addresses wrap
around at 16 bits, so @code{0xffe0} to @code{0xffff} are accepted as
@minus{}32 to @minus{}1, which makes the top of the address space
directly reachable.  After a base register @var{e} may be omitted; if
present, it has to start with @samp{+} or @samp{-}.  @code{$r1} can't
be used as a base register.

@code{$pc} holds the address of the current instruction while the
operand is computed, so a PC-relative reference to @var{label} is
written as @code{[$pc + (@var{label} - .)]}.

The order of the operands selects the destination:

@smallexample
add     $r0, [$sp+1]    ; $r0 = $r0 + mem[$sp+1]
add     [$sp+1], $r0    ; mem[$sp+1] = mem[$sp+1] + $r0
@end smallexample

@node TDM Word Addressing
@section Word Addressing

@cindex TDM word addressing
@cindex word addressing, TDM
The smallest addressable unit on TDM is a 16-bit word.  @code{.}, symbol
values, section sizes and all directive arguments that count bytes count
16-bit words:

@itemize @bullet
@item
@code{.byte}, @code{.short}, @code{.hword} and @code{.word} emit one
word per value; @code{.long} and @code{.int} emit two words, low word
first.

@item
@code{.space}, @code{.skip} and @code{.zero} reserve words.

@item
@code{.align}, @code{.balign}, @code{.balignw} and @code{.p2align}
align to a number of words.

@item
@code{.ascii}, @code{.asciz} and @code{.string} pack two characters per
word, the first character in the low octet, and pad the last word with
a zero octet if needed.
@end itemize

Object files store 16-bit words in little endian octet order.  As with
other ELF targets whose byte is wider than 8 bits, section and segment
headers hold octet addresses, which is what @command{readelf} shows;
@command{objdump} and @command{ld} use word addresses.

DWARF debug information (@option{-g}) is not supported.

@node TDM Opcodes
@section Opcodes

@cindex TDM opcodes
@cindex opcodes for TDM
The instruction set follows the reference assembler of the TDM project.
In the descriptions below @var{reg} is a register and @var{opb} is any
second operand; @var{mem} has to be a memory reference.

@table @code
@item or
@itemx and
@itemx xor
@itemx add
@itemx mov
@code{@var{op} @var{reg}, @var{opb}} or @code{@var{op} [@var{mem}], @var{reg}}.
The first operand is the destination.

@item sub
@itemx isub
@code{sub} computes first operand minus second, @code{isub} second
minus first, in either operand order.  The first operand is the
destination.  With the memory operand first the assembler uses the other
opcode: @code{sub [@var{mem}], @var{reg}} is encoded as an @code{isub}
with a memory destination, and vice versa.

@item swap
@itemx swapi
@code{swap @var{reg}, [@var{mem}]} exchanges a register and a memory
location; the operands may be written in either order.  @code{swapi}
also flips the interrupt disable bit.  Swapping two registers isn't
supported.

@item if_eq
@itemx if_neq
@itemx if_ltu
@itemx if_geu
@itemx if_lts
@itemx if_ges
@itemx if_les
@itemx if_gts
Execute the next instruction only if the condition holds between the
first and the second operand (@samp{u} is unsigned, @samp{s} signed),
otherwise skip it.  With the memory operand first the assembler encodes
the mirrored condition (@code{if_lts [@var{mem}], @var{reg}} becomes
@code{if_gts @var{reg}, [@var{mem}]}).  There are no unsigned @samp{<=}
or @samp{>} conditions, so @code{if_ltu} and @code{if_geu} require the
register operand first.

@item istat
@itemx ror
@itemx rol
@code{@var{op} @var{reg}} or @code{@var{op} [@var{mem}]}.  @code{istat}
stores the interrupt status, @code{ror} and @code{rol} rotate by one bit.

@item brk
@itemx brk @var{n}
A breakpoint, for debuggers and simulators.  Binary operations with a
memory destination but an immediate second operand do nothing, so their
encodings (@code{0b0xxx_1_111_xxxxxxxx}) are reserved for @code{brk}.
The assembler emits @code{0x1f00} plus @var{n} (0 to 255, default 0).
@end table

@node TDM Relocations
@section Relocations

@cindex TDM relocations
@cindex relocations, TDM
@table @code
@item R_TDM_16
@itemx R_TDM_32
16- and 32-bit data, emitted by @code{.word} and @code{.long}.
@item R_TDM_IMM6
The 6-bit immediate field of an instruction, with the same 16-bit wrap
around as above.
@item R_TDM_PCREL6
The 6-bit immediate field, relative to the address of the instruction.
@code{[$pc + (@var{label} - .)]} produces this relocation when
@var{label} is in another section or file.
@end table
